#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Judge whether the company host package installs and runs on each Linux family.

`tools/test-native-install` judges the Debian package end to end with a company
on a local plane. This one serves the directory `internkim release packages`
writes the way GitHub serves a release, runs the published `install.sh` line on a
guest of any family the package is built for (Debian, Ubuntu, Fedora,
RHEL-compatible, Arch Linux), and reads the guest's own state: the package
database's, systemd's, the filesystem's. A package or a SHA256SUMS changed after
the release was written has to be refused, and running the line again has to
leave the machine on the release, which is how a host is upgraded. It is the
test of the same payload as a deb, an rpm and a pacman package, portable
maintainer scripts, and the host's own PostgreSQL and cache on Unix sockets.

What it does not do is run a company on a real plane. The guest is given a
connection file whose addresses go nowhere, so the gateway, the record and the
messenger round trip are `tools/test-native-install`'s to judge. What this reads
instead is everything that touches the database and the cache: the two units,
the accounts, the modes, the sockets, the database schema, and the clients that open
them.

    tools/test-native-install-family --help
"""

